+### §check-fails-open — the check aborts with a non-zero exit when one of its own commands fails
+- Since: 2026-10-22 (overridden by the maintainer; replaces the 2026-09-11 decision below)
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit. The large-file report is
+  still only a warning: finding large files does not change the exit status, which stays 0.
+- Forbidden: `cmd && action` as a conditional where it can be the last command in a loop or function. Under `-e`, a
+  false test there becomes a non-zero exit (`[ "$kb" -gt "$limit_kb" ] && echo` exited 1 whenever the last staged
+  file was under the limit). Write `if …; then …; fi` instead.
+- Reason: the maintainer overrode the fail-open rule on 2026-10-22 so that internal errors are visible instead of
+  silently passing. The staged-then-deleted file that caused the 2026-09-10 outage is skipped by the `[ -f "$f" ]`
+  guard before `wc` runs.
+- Superseded (2026-09-11 → 2026-10-22): `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`, always `exit 0`, `set -e`/`errexit`
+  forbidden. Reason then: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked
+  every commit in the repository for a day (2026-09-10).
